// @run
// @log fld
// @hint redundant type annotation: `b` is inferred as `{tag: () => string}` — remove it to lean on inference
// AN UNBOUNDED `<T>` IS AN ENTIRELY OPEN HOLE (owner ruling, 2026-09-03;
// `docs/constraints-design.md` §7.7). A member CALL on one tries the bounded-member
// route, then UFCS dispatch by name, and — when both come up empty — derives the
// member the way an inference hole does, deferring the check to each call.
//
// THE SPELLING THIS PROVES IS THE ONE NOBODY WOULD WRITE: no free `self`-function
// called `tag` anywhere in the program, so there is no precedence question at all.
// Every other fixture in the field-dispatch family carries one, because that family
// is ABOUT the precedence, and this shape is the one none of them could see (D1221).
//
// The un-annotated twin — `function g(x) { x.tag() }` — prints `fld` too, and that is
// the whole ruling: the two spellings of one generic answer alike.
type Boxed = { tag: () => string }

function g<T>(x: T): string {
  x.tag()
}

const b: Boxed = { tag: () => "fld" }
print(g(b))
